<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ §01 ══ -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Memory Wall</div>
<h2>90% of GPU Memory Is Wasted on Idle History</h2>
<p>
  Modern LLMs can attend to a million tokens — but <em>paying to store</em> all those tokens in fast GPU memory is a different question. The KV cache (the stored keys and values for every past token) grows <strong>linearly with context length</strong>. A 500 K token conversation holds roughly 1.87 GB of compressed KV data in the DeepSeek-V4-Flash architecture alone — and that's after all of DeepSeek's aggressive compression tricks.
</p>
<p>
  The waste is eye-opening: the authors audited real inference logs and found that <strong>over 90% of requests with contexts &gt; 64 K tokens</strong> can be answered using only the last 8 K tokens. The historical context sits in expensive HBM memory doing nothing.
</p>
<p>
  The obvious fix — a sliding window that discards old context — catastrophically fails on the remaining 10% of queries that genuinely need global recall (like "which document mentioned X 400 K tokens ago?"). You need both: fast local access <em>and</em> on-demand global recall, without paying for both simultaneously.
</p>

<!-- Interactive: memory scaling canvas -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">KV cache memory vs context length — four strategies</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Full cache grows linearly; LSA stays flat</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="mem-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.75rem;margin-top:.75rem;justify-content:center">
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem;font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#ef4444;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block"></span>DS-V4-Flash (full cache)</span>
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem;font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#8b5cf6;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block"></span>FM-DS-V4 (LSA)</span>
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem;font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#6b7280;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block"></span>Recency Only</span>
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem;font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#06b6d4;flex-shrink:0;display:inline-block"></span>Random 10%</span>
    </div>
    <div id="mem-note" style="text-align:center;margin-top:.5rem;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">hover over a data point to see exact values</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The chart makes the trade-off visible: <strong>Recency Only and Random 10%</strong> keep memory constant by discarding history, but their accuracy collapses (from 76.9% to 33–38%). <strong>FlashMemory</strong> achieves near-constant memory (≈10× smaller than full cache) by fetching only what's predicted to matter — and ends up <em>more accurate</em> than keeping everything.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ §02 ══ -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">02 — Three-Tier Memory</div>
<h2>DeepSeek's Hybrid Architecture Makes This Possible</h2>
<p>
  FlashMemory is built on DeepSeek-V4's hybrid attention design, which already separates attention into multiple tiers. Understanding this layering is essential — it's what makes the memory indexer practical.
</p>

<!-- Architecture SVG -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">FM-DS-V4 three-tier memory architecture</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">GPU hot-pool + CPU cold-pool + predictive indexer</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body arch-svg">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 660 320" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="font-family:var(--ff-mono);font-size:11px">
      <!-- GPU hot pool -->
      <rect x="20" y="20" width="280" height="270" rx="10" fill="rgba(139,92,246,.07)" stroke="rgba(139,92,246,.35)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
      <text x="160" y="44" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" letter-spacing="1">GPU HOT POOL</text>

      <!-- Tier 1: HCA -->
      <rect x="36" y="56" width="248" height="52" rx="6" fill="rgba(139,92,246,.18)" stroke="rgba(139,92,246,.5)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <text x="160" y="77" fill="#c4b5fd" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">HCA Chunks (full context)</text>
      <text x="160" y="94" fill="#6b7280" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">128:1 compression · always resident</text>

      <!-- Tier 2: Sliding window -->
      <rect x="36" y="120" width="248" height="52" rx="6" fill="rgba(16,185,129,.12)" stroke="rgba(16,185,129,.4)" stroke-width="1"/>
      <text x="160" y="141" fill="#6ee7b7" font-weight="600" text-anchor="middle">Sliding Window (last 8K + decoded)</text>
      <text x="160" y="158" fill="#6b7280" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">fine-grained · always resident</text>

      <!-- Tier 3: Fetched CSA -->
