<p>
  The three-tier design separates concerns cleanly:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>HCA chunks</strong> (128:1 compression) carry the global semantic signal and are always resident in GPU — they're cheap enough to keep.</li>
  <li><strong>Sliding window</strong> (last 8 K + actively decoded tokens) handles fine-grained local recall, always in GPU.</li>
  <li><strong>CSA chunks</strong> (the bulk of the history, fine-grained) live on CPU and are paged into GPU only when predicted to be needed — this is where FlashMemory's savings come from.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  The key insight is that CSA chunks are already compressed by DeepSeek's architecture, and their <em>indexer keys</em> K<sup>IComp</sup> are fixed — they don't change during inference. This frozen representation is exactly what makes decoupled training feasible.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ §03 ══ -->
<section id="s-indexer" data-code="indexer">
<div class="section-no">03 — Lookahead Indexer</div>
<h2>Predicting Future Context Needs From the Present Hidden State</h2>
<p>
  The central algorithmic contribution is the <strong>Memory Indexer</strong> — a tiny dual-encoder that, given the current hidden state h<sub>t</sub>, predicts which historical CSA chunks will be needed during the next τ decoding steps.
</p>
<p>
  Every τ=64 steps the indexer runs: it projects h<sub>t</sub> into low-rank query vectors across n<sub>lh</sub> indexer heads, computes a head-fused gated matching score against all compressed historical keys, and applies a <strong>Sigmoid threshold</strong> (≥ 0.5) to decide which chunks to fetch. Crucially it does <em>not</em> use a fixed Top-k — the number of fetched chunks adapts to how many are actually needed.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Lookahead Score (Eq. 4)</div>
  <div><span class="math-var">I</span><sub>t,s</sub> = σ( Σ<sub>h</sub> <span class="math-var">w</span><sup>l</sup><sub>t,h</sub> · ReLU( <span class="math-var">q</span><sup>l</sup><sub>t,h</sub>ᵀ · <span class="math-var">K</span><sup>IComp</sup><sub>s</sub> ) )</div>
  <div style="margin-top:.35rem;font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted)">
    σ = Sigmoid · w<sup>l</sup><sub>t,h</sub> = learned routing head weight · K<sup>IComp</sup><sub>s</sub> = frozen compressed key of chunk s
  </div>
  <div style="margin-top:.35rem;font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted)">
    → fetch chunk s if I<sub>t,s</sub> ≥ 0.5 (dynamic recall, not fixed Top-k)
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Three strategically chosen intermediate layers (10, 12, and 20) run independent indexers; any chunk that passes the 0.5 threshold in <em>at least one</em> layer is fetched (OR-mode routing). Early layers are excluded — their representations encode token statistics, not semantic dependencies.
</p>

<!-- Step navigator: data curation pipeline -->
<h3>How are the training labels created?</h3>
<p>
  The trickiest part is defining the ground-truth "golden chunks" for each query token window. A naïve Top-k label produces ~10,000 positives per window — far too noisy. The paper uses a <strong>Cross-Layer Majority Voting</strong> pipeline to isolate true contextual backbone entries:
</p>

<div class="step-list" id="step-list">
  <div class="step-box" data-step="0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Softmax Normalization</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each decoding token i and each of the L=21 CSA layers, convert raw indexer logit scores over all history chunks into a probability distribution via Softmax. This puts all layers on a common scale.</div>
      <div class="step-code">P_i,l,s = exp(S_i,l,s) / Σ_j exp(S_i,l,j)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Top-p Thresholding (Nucleus, p=0.6)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Instead of a fixed Top-k count, retain only entries that collectively account for the top 60% of probability mass. This yields a variable-size, high-confidence set M_i,l for each layer — eliminating long-tail noise entries.</div>
      <div class="step-code">M_i,l = &#123;s | cumulative P ≤ 0.6 from highest to lowest&#125;</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Cross-Layer Majority Voting (θ=3)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Count how many of the 21 layers independently selected entry s. Only entries with ≥ 3 votes are declared "golden." This consensus filters out layer-specific noise — a true contextual dependency should be visible across multiple layers.</div>
      <div class="step-code">A_i^golden = &#123;s | Σ_l I(s ∈ M_i,l) ≥ 3&#125;</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Temporal Union (lookahead window τ=64)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Union all golden entries across the τ=64 future steps [t, t+τ-1]. The result is Y⁺_t — the set of historical chunks that at least one step in the lookahead window genuinely needs. Label size drops from ~10,000 to ~100–1,000 per window.</div>
      <div class="step-code">Y⁺_t = ∪_&#123;i=t&#125;^&#123;t+τ-1&#125; A_i^golden</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="step-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ §04 ══ -->
<section id="s-training" data-code="training">
<div class="section-no">04 — Decoupled Training</div>
<h2>Training in One GPU-Hour Without Loading the Backbone</h2>
<p>
  The most surprising engineering result is how cheaply the indexer is trained. Normally, adapting any component of a trillion-parameter LLM requires loading the full backbone for forward passes — measured in days on a large GPU cluster. FlashMemory sidesteps this entirely.
</p>

<!-- Before / after contrast -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Training cost: standard fine-tuning vs FlashMemory decoupled training</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">One-GPU-hour vs full-cluster fine-tune</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="train-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The trick is that the compressed indexer keys K<sup>IComp</sup> are <strong>pre-computed and frozen</strong>. During training, the indexer never needs to compute attention over the full sequence — it only needs the hidden state h<sub>t</sub> (pre-extracted) and the frozen keys. The trainable components are just three small projection matrices:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>W<sup>DQ</sup></strong> — down-projection for the lookahead query (d → d<sub>c</sub>)</li>
  <li><strong>W<sup>IUQ</sup></strong> — up-projection for per-head queries (d<sub>c</sub> → c·n<sub>lh</sub>)</li>
  <li><strong>W<sup>w</sup></strong> — routing head weights (d → n<sub>lh</sub>)</li>
</ul>
<p>
  These amount to &lt; 0.1% of the full model's parameters. The optimization objective is a Focal Loss with BCE — standard binary classification. The class imbalance (positive chunks ≈ 0.1–1% of history) is handled by a 3:1 negative sampling ratio plus sample weighting, not a separate α term.
</p>

<div class="kv-callout">
  <div class="kv-num">500</div>
  <div class="kv-desc">Distinct training runs executed in a single week on 8× H20 GPUs — possible only because each run takes ~1 GPU-hour. Under standard joint distillation, each run would take days.</div>
</div>

<h3>Optimal layer configuration</h3>
<p>
  Not all layers are equally useful for lookahead prediction. Shallow layers encode token-level statistics (not helpful). Deeper layers carry global semantic dependencies — but using too many layers (8-layer ensemble) causes the recall mask to become too loose: 30–49% of chunks get fetched, defeating the purpose.
</p>
<p>
  After a 500-run Pareto sweep, the optimal configuration is <strong>exactly three layers: 10, 12, and 20</strong>. Their union (OR-mode) delivers robust fallback protection without excessive over-fetching.
</p>
</section>

