<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ §06 ══ -->
<section id="s-limits" data-code="limits">
<div class="section-no">06 — Limits</div>
<h2>Two Hard Failure Modes to Know About</h2>

<h3>1. Dense global memory (MRCR benchmark)</h3>
<p>
  The MRCR (Multi-Range Context Retrieval) benchmark requires attending to many dispersed locations simultaneously. FM-DS-V4 drops from 76.0% → 48.0% — a severe regression.
</p>
<p>
  The root cause, confirmed via oracle simulation: most benchmarks only need 10–25% of the true golden CSA chunks to match full-context accuracy. MRCR needs &gt;50% — the compressed dual-encoder simply can't predict all of them. A ColBERT-style late-interaction architecture would help but requires the backbone to be involved in training.
</p>

<h3>2. Length generalization ceiling</h3>
<p>
  The indexer generalizes safely to 2× its training context length but collapses beyond that. The indexer was trained on contexts up to 512K, so it's expected to work at 1M — but the authors were unable to test this. The failure mode is positional OOD: the indexer's positional embeddings diverge from what the attention layers expect at extreme lengths.
</p>

<!-- KV callout: the three known architectural limits -->
<div class="kv-callout">
  <div class="kv-num">3</div>
  <div class="kv-desc">Known architectural limits: (1) frozen K<sup>IComp</sup> keys — never fine-tuned; (2) shallow dot-product interaction — no late-interaction cross-matching; (3) decoupled training — no end-to-end gradient from the live autoregressive shift. Each is a future research direction.</div>
</div>

<h3>The "less is more" regime and its boundaries</h3>
<p>
  FlashMemory's accuracy gains come from filtering out noisy context — but this only works when the "true" context is sparse. When the task genuinely requires dense global memory (MRCR), the indexer fails. The paper's honest assessment: the current system is the first glimpse of what LSA can achieve under severe resource constraints — frozen keys, shallow interaction, no joint optimization — and the direction is clearly promising.
</p>
</section>

